{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["A man, when there shall be in the skin of his flesh — all the passages about skin-afflictions of a person and afflictions of garments and afflictions of houses, and their appearances and the reckoning of their quarantine periods and white hairs and black hair and yellow hair — we have nothing at all beyond the midrash of the Sages, nor any expertise in the worldly ways of human beings; rather, the midrash of the Sages and their statutes and their traditions received from the earlier Sages — that is the essence." "When there shall be in the skin of his flesh — since it will be said further on: כי יהיה בו נגע בראש או בזקן, it was necessary here to say: in the skin of his flesh." "שאת — a term of elevation. It is not as white as a בהרת, but the בהרת is white, as written: בהיר הוא בשחקים — and it appears deeper than [the שאת / the בהרת], like the appearance of sunlight being deeper than shadow." "ספחת — an offshoot of שאת. Or an offshoot of בהרת. A derivative of שאת. And there is also a derivative of בהרת that combines with it, as written: ספחני נא אל אחת הכהונות — a term of attachment and combining. Thus the Sages explained." "For the affliction of tzara'at — that the place of the affliction should be white flesh, as written: מצורעת כשלג. Thus the affliction is called this when it is white."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {2 "The bracketed reading note in the source (מבהרת → צ״ל מעור/משאת) indicates textual uncertainty about the comparison; rendered to preserve Rashbam's point about relative depth of appearance."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:48:48.674379519Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}